## What it does

`packages/turbo-cache` owns a separate Worker, a dedicated R2 bucket, and one generated credential per configured team.
The Stack includes the [cartridge](/lore/cartridges) with `yield* turboCache`.
The package README describes configuration and removal.

Turbo sends binary task archives through its own HTTP protocol.
The Worker streams those bodies to R2 and streams stored bodies back.
It preserves artifact duration, optional signature tags, and byte lengths.

This is an external protocol [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture).
The [capability projection](/lore/one-capability-every-surface) requires struct inputs, excludes HEAD, and does not describe raw upload streams with per-artifact response headers.
The adapter adds no application capability or alternate agent route.

R2 stores disposable copies of CI outputs under `team/hash` keys.
Turbo's task hash determines reuse.
CI remains responsible for the task result.

## The standard

Every supported route requires a bearer, including status and event acknowledgement.
The Worker compares fixed-length SHA-256 digests with Workers' native constant-time comparison.
It checks every configured credential before selecting a match.

Each token authorizes one team prefix.
A missing or wrong bearer gets 401.
A valid bearer with an unknown or different team gets 403.

Other routes return 404.
There are no internal, debug, populate, count, or expiry-override routes.

A credential holder can replace artifacts within its team.
Team separation does not protect writers from another writer in the same team.
Only trusted CI should receive credentials.
Turbo owns optional signature verification; the Worker preserves the signature tag without verifying archive contents.

Main uses `local:w,remote:w`, so it never consumes artifacts written by a pull request.
Pull requests use `local:rw,remote:rw`.
The nightly and manual main canary also disable all cache reads.

A red canary beside green cached checks calls for inspecting task inputs and environment declarations.
Other test or runner failures still need diagnosis.

Retention starts at upload.
Reads do not extend an artifact's lifetime.
The default is seven days, configured through an R2 lifecycle rule.
Deletion is asynchronous.

There is no listing cron and no application data in this bucket.

GET responses disable edge caching.
If edge caching is added, deletes will not instantly invalidate those cached responses.
The Worker, bucket, and generated tokens retain only in production.

`TURBO_API` is the origin without `/v8`.
`TURBO_TEAM` is the team slug.
Remote caching stays off unless both token and team are set.

Turbo warns and executes tasks when the cache is unreachable.
A missing token also leaves tasks running without remote reuse.

## How to check

Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/turbo-cache test`.
Generated runtime checks cover byte-identical reads, team isolation, HEAD agreement, and auth before storage.
The real Turbo seam uses two empty local cache directories and requires a REMOTE hit on its second run.

The credential handoff reads completed [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy) state in process.
Run `pnpm turbo-cache:token --team rat-stack --stage prod -- <trusted-command>` after the approved deployment.
Only that command's stdin receives the token.

Its stdout and stderr are suppressed.
The parent prints only the API origin and team slug after success.
The desk owns the authorized GitHub secret write.

CI uploads `.turbo/runs/*.json` to separate LOCAL and REMOTE hits.
Check those summaries alongside task counts and the uncached main canary.
Run the full gate with `pnpm turbo run check test build --concurrency=4` before shipping.

Local workerd tests do not prove deployed account access, lifecycle deletion, or GitHub credential delivery.
Those remain live checks for the deployment owner.
No deployment is claimed by this page.
